From: fix_nofeed_no_stop <maintainer@example.com>
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: dvb-core: dvb_net: demote "no feed to stop" to debug

The functions dvb_net_feed_stop() is called on every ndo_stop (interface
down) and on every rx-mode / multicast change via wq_set_multicast_list().
When the network interface has no IP feed actually running (secfeed / tsfeed
== NULL), it logs at KERN_ERR:

    dvb_net: dvb0_0: no feed to stop
    dvb_net: dvb1_0: no ts feed to stop
    ...

On multi-tuner cards (e.g. TBS6909x + TBS6916) this creates dozens of
interfaces (dvb0_0 .. dvb27_0). A network manager that periodically probes
or reconfigures every interface then floods the kernel log with these
lines. The condition is entirely benign: there was simply nothing to stop.

Demote both messages from pr_err() to netdev_dbg() so they no longer spam
the ring buffer, while remaining available via dynamic debug when needed.
netdev_dbg() also prepends the interface name, so drop the manual "%s".

Signed-off-by: fix_nofeed_no_stop <maintainer@example.com>
---
 drivers/media/dvb-core/dvb_net.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/dvb-core/dvb_net.c b/drivers/media/dvb-core/dvb_net.c
--- a/drivers/media/dvb-core/dvb_net.c
+++ b/drivers/media/dvb-core/dvb_net.c
@@ -1108,7 +1108,7 @@ static int dvb_net_feed_stop(struct net_device *dev)
 			priv->demux->release_section_feed(priv->demux, priv->secfeed);
 			priv->secfeed = NULL;
 		} else
-			pr_err("%s: no feed to stop\n", dev->name);
+			netdev_dbg(dev, "no feed to stop\n");
 	} else if (priv->feedtype == DVB_NET_FEEDTYPE_ULE) {
 		if (priv->tsfeed) {
 			if (priv->tsfeed->is_filtering) {
@@ -1120,7 +1120,7 @@ static int dvb_net_feed_stop(struct net_device *dev)
 			priv->tsfeed = NULL;
 		}
 		else
-			pr_err("%s: no ts feed to stop\n", dev->name);
+			netdev_dbg(dev, "no ts feed to stop\n");
 	} else
 		ret = -EINVAL;
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->mutex);
--
2.40.0
